Complex / Chronic Wound Management
General Practice Other
Skin defect failing to heal >4 weeks, Presence of slough/eschar/exudate, Assessment of underlying vascular supply, Wound swab culture

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-008
Reason for Visit: Acute infectious deterioration of a chronic lower limb ulcer with sudden onset of severe pain and malodorous purulent discharge.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an elderly patient with a known chronic venous ulcer experiencing a rapid, 12-hour escalation in localized pain, sudden heavy purulent exudate, and surrounding erythema. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to differentiate localized wound infection from rapidly spreading cellulitis or sepsis, perform immediate wound debridement, and initiate systemic antibiotics. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period risked systemic septicemia and the potential need for major surgical amputation.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My leg ulcer has suddenly become agonizing today, it smells terrible, and the bandage is soaked with green fluid."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a documented presence of a skin defect failing to progress through expected healing phases over 4 weeks on the left medial malleolus. Over the past 12 hours, there has been a dramatic shift in wound characteristics. Exudate production has transitioned rapidly from minimal serous fluid to heavy purulence. The patient reports a sudden, severe escalation in localized pain, making dressing changes intolerable. There is a distinct, new malodour originating from the wound site. Nursing staff noted the sudden appearance of surrounding skin erythema and warmth.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to the acute, severe pain upon dependency of the limb.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid systemic deterioration given the patient's advanced age and compromised vascular status.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 110/65 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Appearing fatigued, visibly wincing when the left leg is moved, low-grade fever present.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the left lower limb reveals a 5cm x 4cm ulcer. There is visualization of slough and necrotic tissue within the wound bed, with an absence of healthy pink granulation tissue. There is a 4cm margin of surrounding skin erythema, warmth, and induration indicating localized infection. Assessment of the wound dimensions and depth reveals no gross undermining. Evaluation of underlying contributing factors confirms severe venous insufficiency with hemosiderin staining. The clinical presentation strongly features a skin defect failing to heal greater than 4 weeks, the presence of slough, eschar, and exudate, requiring assessment of the underlying vascular supply and a wound swab culture.
Environmental Observations: Wound care supplies appropriately stocked in the room; strong malodor present upon entering.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1g QID verified; no current antibiotic therapy.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Complex / Chronic Wound Management (Acute Localized Wound Infection) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain and erythema are strictly localized circumferentially around the open wound, without generalized unilateral calf swelling or a positive Homan's sign.

Necrotizing Fasciitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of palpable crepitus, hemorrhagic bullae, pain out of proportion to the visual clinical findings, or profound systemic shock.

Systemic Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Only mild tachycardia and a low-grade fever, with a maintained blood pressure and normal mentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient demonstrated acute clinical deterioration localized to a chronic wound, featuring a new-onset fever, heavy purulence, and severe localized pain. Telephone triage correctly identified the high risk of advancing infection in a compromised host. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was mandatory to remove the saturated dressings, inspect the wound bed for deep tissue involvement, obtain microbiological samples, and commence targeted empiric antibiotic therapy. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have permitted the localized infection to advance to systemic bacteremia, inevitably requiring emergency hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle mechanical debridement of loose slough. Collected a deep tissue wound swab for microscopy, culture, and sensitivities (positive identification of heavy bacterial load expected). Applied an antimicrobial silver-based dressing. Prescribed oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for 7 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate optimal wound healing and reduce dependency edema, elevate the left leg on two pillows strictly whenever resting in bed. Pair the act of taking your new antibiotic tablet with immediately elevating your leg. Linking the new medication routine with the physical act of elevation establishes a consistent, twice-daily habit that actively combats venous stasis.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Updated the nursing wound care directive in the facility chart.
DME/Safety (Optional): Foam wedges for leg elevation.
Follow-up: Facility GP review required within 48 hours to check swab results and clinical response to antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Facility staff to arrange immediate ED transfer via 000 if the patient becomes acutely confused, if the systolic blood pressure drops below 90, or if the redness spreads rapidly up the calf within hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute pain and heavy discharge should begin to improve within 48 hours of starting the antibiotics. The underlying ulcer will require long-term management.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Notify the nursing staff immediately if you feel sudden, uncontrollable shivering, or if the pain becomes worse despite the new dressing and pain relief.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The chronic wound has developed a new bacterial infection. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria, and the silver dressing will draw out the infected fluid.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics strictly with food. Keep your leg elevated above your heart whenever you are in bed to reduce the throbbing pressure.

